    "summary": "Hot Help>Slipknot>Franklin's run & powerful second set",
    "blurb": "The show shines with a smoking Help On The Way → Slipknot → Franklin's Tower sequence and a high‑energy second half that rolls through Samson, China Cat Sunflower, Looks Like Rain and The Wheel. Jerry’s lead work is strong throughout, while the rhythm section keeps the groove tight. Recording quality varies, but the best soundboard capture delivers crystal‑clear detail.",
    "review": "On March 24, 1991 at Albany’s Knickerbocker Arena the Dead delivered a marathon 165‑minute set that still feels fresh. The first set kicks off with an unforgettable Help On The Way → Slipknot → Franklin's Tower medley—Jerry’s ragged yet gorgeous voice and Vince’s forward‑leaning keyboards cut through the mix, while Bob Weir adds his signature rhythm chops. Highlights like Deal, Brown Eyed Women and a soaring Desolation Row keep the momentum rolling despite a few uneven moments.\nThe second set is where the night truly ignites: Samson & Delilah segues into China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider before launching into Looks Like Rain, He’s Gone, an expansive Drum‑Space excursion, then The Wheel → I Need A Miracle → Standing On The Moon → Good Lovin'. Mickey and Billy provide deep, room‑vibrating percussion that fuels the 90s vibe, and Vince’s solos add a bright edge. The encore’s U.S. Blues rounds out an evening that, while not flawless, offers plenty of classic Dead magic for any collector.",
